1
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:07,790
This is Software Engineering Radio, the podcast for professional developers on the web at se-radio.net.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:18,590
SE Radio is brought to you by the IEEE Computer Society and by IEEE Software Magazine online at computer.org/software.

3
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:23,630
Hello everyone. [clears throat] Welcome to today's episode of Software Engineering Radio.

4
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:25,590
Our guest is Sonali Verde.

5
00:00:25,590 --> 00:00:27,830
Sonali is senior software engineering manager at LinkedIn.

6
00:00:27,830 --> 00:00:36,910
She has more than two decades of experience leading engineering teams across architecture, product dev, and platform deliveries.

7
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:42,750
Today, we're going to chat with Sonali about how AI is changing the engineering manager role.

8
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:49,910
Sonali, before we begin, I should note that I know you through my network and have followed your career for some time.

9
00:00:49,910 --> 00:00:58,830
I think you're particularly well qualified for this conversation given your experience as well as LinkedIn's early adoption of AI.

10
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:00,910
That said, is there anything you'd like to add to your bio before we start?

11
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:06,510
Hi Kanchan, happy to be here on this podcast with you.

12
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:15,390
The only other thing I would add is that I also love to mentor and coach early career managers and walk them through some of my journeys and lessons.

13
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:18,390
Okay, let's start with your own job.

14
00:01:18,390 --> 00:01:25,350
If I followed you around for a week, where would I actually see AI showing up in your work?

15
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:27,670
All right.

16
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:34,950
I think if you followed me through the week, I think my first meeting of the week starts with like an operational review.

17
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:46,630
We have a lot of systems, legacy and new, that we own and a lot of time goes in making sure that we have like a right level of operational rigor.

18
00:01:46,630 --> 00:01:49,790
And so, the first early signs of where

19
00:01:49,790 --> 00:01:52,790
I needed to have automation was actually

20
00:01:52,790 --> 00:02:04,630
having lot of my operational dashboards automated in a way that I could synthesize that information for my leadership as well as my VP level org.

21
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:12,150
And so if you followed me, you will see that every week there is like what are the new signals, what are the new insights,

22
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:17,990
what are the new experiments that the team launched or did.

23
00:02:17,990 --> 00:02:23,830
And so a lot of time goes into understanding some of those operational nuances and

24
00:02:23,830 --> 00:02:28,270
that's where you would see me using a lot of AI skills or

25
00:02:28,270 --> 00:02:34,590
trying to automate things in a way that's easier for my team to understand and

26
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:39,949
digest what signals we should be debugging more.

27
00:02:39,949 --> 00:02:48,990
And two is like for me to uplevel how do I communicate some of the things that I am seeing in a way that can be summarized and synthesized better for my leadership.

28
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,190
Can you just talk about what specifically changed here because of AI?

29
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:57,350
I'm assuming a lot of the automation was in place.

30
00:02:57,350 --> 00:02:58,910
You talked about summarization.

31
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:02,390
Perhaps that's an example, but what else changed due to AI?

32
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:05,150
So you have lots of dashboards, right?

33
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:13,351
And so those dashboards do have multiple systems that have different kinds of dashboards and tooling. Right?

34
00:03:13,351 --> 00:03:22,670
If you are on a legacy system, the legacy system has like a different kind of automation and a tool output that is available for you.

35
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:29,710
Then you are on newer systems and newer systems have probably been on boarded to like a newer tool and things like that.

36
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:35,270
And so there was a lot of switching and making sure that we were taking data from multiple tools,

37
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:45,750
multiple places and then trying to create our own visual or like a spreadsheet of how do we understand the metrics that matter.

38
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:51,390
So the first thing that I was able to do is actually build like a cloud skill, right?

39
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:59,430
That can now go through these different systems, understands what exactly from that output I'm looking for,

40
00:03:59,430 --> 00:04:06,150
and creating this daily summary, if you will, for me in a way that A,

41
00:04:06,150 --> 00:04:13,070
I don't have to run like this 1-hour review meetings with multiple managers coming and talking about their systems.

42
00:04:13,070 --> 00:04:16,229
And so, it's reduced the meeting time.

43
00:04:16,229 --> 00:04:21,789
It reduced a lot of conversations about was there data available or not.

44
00:04:21,789 --> 00:04:24,150
All of those things happened in the background.

45
00:04:24,150 --> 00:04:33,790
And when we came into the meeting, we were actually able to focus on the real insights that were the problem for that particular operational review.

46
00:04:33,790 --> 00:04:41,630
And so, it just up-leveled a little bit in terms of where we used to start the operational review to where we are now.

47
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:44,431
Thanks, Ali. That's helpful.

48
00:04:44,431 --> 00:04:52,430
For people that may be not too familiar yet with Claude skills, can you explain what that is?

49
00:04:52,430 --> 00:04:58,990
And then, maybe get into a little bit of a detail on how exactly you use that here, and why that was not possible before AI.

50
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,670
I would say that it's not that it wasn't possible.

51
00:05:04,670 --> 00:05:08,790
It needed to have that level of rigor of figuring out what the current job should be.

52
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:13,870
And there was a little bit of the grind that was never prioritized.

53
00:05:13,870 --> 00:05:24,030
And once we got access to Claude, Claude actually had like an easier way to onboard all of these things in a way that you could speak to it as like a natural language, right?

54
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:28,710
Like you can talk about it as if like, "Hey, these are the things that I do.

55
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:31,710
Here are the links that I go to.

56
00:05:31,710 --> 00:05:37,230
And here's what I actually look for as an output."

57
00:05:37,230 --> 00:05:39,870
And it's almost like a chief of staff kind of a skill.

58
00:05:39,870 --> 00:05:43,030
If there was somebody who could help me with a task,

59
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:54,670
it's literally like Claude skill is almost like you are telling someone the actual task in a way that you would explain it to another human.

60
00:05:54,670 --> 00:05:59,190
And then it actually says, "Okay, here's how I understand you.

61
00:05:59,190 --> 00:06:03,870
Here's what I think is the output that you're looking for, and here's what I can do."

62
00:06:03,870 --> 00:06:10,990
And then builds a skill that you can then say, "Okay, run this every day morning, or run this before my ops meeting,

63
00:06:10,990 --> 00:06:16,870
and then send out an email on the data that is missing to my leaders, or whatever, right?

64
00:06:16,870 --> 00:06:24,030
It has a way that once you have like the basic skill, you are actually able to talk to it and make it even better.

65
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:29,790
How does this compare with prompts that were all the rage a year ago?

66
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:41,670
I think when we were thinking about prompts, we were talking a little bit more of using them for solving customer kind of a problem.

67
00:06:41,670 --> 00:06:51,510
Like we were thinking about prompts and AI of like, "Hey, how can this help in solving something that is more of like uh economics or a business problem, right?"

68
00:06:51,520 --> 00:07:02,230
We weren't as focused as what we would do with it from like an internal developer productivity or coding kind of tasks.

69
00:07:02,230 --> 00:07:11,310
We were thinking about coding kind of tasks, but they were a lot more of as if a step up of like an IntelliJ or an Eclipse automation.

70
00:07:11,310 --> 00:07:19,350
We were not thinking about it from the power that it could have of just automating like end-to-end workflows.

71
00:07:19,350 --> 00:07:24,670
We were thinking of them in a lot more compartmentalized way is what I would say.

72
00:07:24,670 --> 00:07:31,190
Even with IntelliJ or Eclipse, you had like an automation of boilerplate code, and we just thought,

73
00:07:31,190 --> 00:07:38,470
"Hey, with the prompts, we could go one step further and have something that's a little bit more built out, right?

74
00:07:38,470 --> 00:07:41,510
Or template-based kind of things."

75
00:07:41,510 --> 00:07:46,150
So, we were thinking a lot more smaller, I would say, than how we are thinking about it now.

76
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:59,110
Sounds like it's positive, but do you have any example where the AI told you, in this case you're using Claude, so AI told you that something looked useful and convincing, but was actually wrong?

77
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:04,950
Yeah, it tells you what you should be doing.

78
00:08:04,950 --> 00:08:10,430
At the same time, you still have to use human judgment and look yourself for some of the depth.

79
00:08:10,430 --> 00:08:18,270
I definitely, as I was trying to adopt some of these tools and see where and how I could coach my team members to use it.

80
00:08:18,270 --> 00:08:25,510
I used it in writing or bug fixing some of UX bugs.

81
00:08:25,510 --> 00:08:33,950
And a lot of times, I do come from a back-end engineering background, and so there are things like accessibility, there are things related to resizing,

82
00:08:33,960 --> 00:08:44,150
reflows, kinds of architecture, where I just took some of the inputs that the AI agent gave me,

83
00:08:44,150 --> 00:08:51,750
and I implemented it only to find out during the code review from my tech leads that this is not the right implementation.

84
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:54,030
There was really no bug in the regular flow.

85
00:08:54,030 --> 00:08:59,390
The bug actually happened in a reflow kind of an architecture of this UX.

86
00:08:59,390 --> 00:09:07,790
And so, I actually learned that even though you could use some of the AI tooling, you have to still have human in the loop.

87
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:09,790
You need to understand the context.

88
00:09:09,790 --> 00:09:12,870
You need to understand the depth.

89
00:09:12,870 --> 00:09:15,150
The skills of like hard debugging,

90
00:09:15,150 --> 00:09:23,670
the skill of actually having the context in which you are looking at the problem is supremely important,

91
00:09:23,670 --> 00:09:30,070
and that's my biggest learning with using some of these agents in my day-to-day.

92
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:36,150
How I make decisions about when should I trust it completely versus when should I trust, but go with a caution?

93
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:45,430
So, understanding is key, even though you are getting the AI's help in synthesizing and perhaps thinking to a large extent.

94
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:47,270
Yes, absolutely.

95
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:54,590
So, you did give examples where you're using Claude in this case to help you

96
00:09:54,600 --> 00:10:08,110
work through what metrics should be used, in this case also to do a bug fix, which you felt was a good start, but couldn't replace human judgment and understanding.

97
00:10:08,110 --> 00:10:15,670
Are you also as a manager using AI to actually, as an agent, to do things beyond suggesting code changes, for example?

98
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:24,350
So, the places where my team is actually using is creating like agents for automation of some of our workflows.

99
00:10:24,350 --> 00:10:32,550
One of the examples that we recently had is we do have like when some feature is ready for bug bashing,

100
00:10:32,550 --> 00:10:38,150
we go through like a bug bashing sheet, we come up with which platforms are people testing,

101
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:48,150
and then we log all of these bugs in a spreadsheet first, and then we import that spreadsheet into our Jira workflows.

102
00:10:48,150 --> 00:10:50,750
One of the things we did was we just created a bug bash agent, right?

103
00:10:50,760 --> 00:11:02,910
So, as the cross-functional team comes together for an end-to-end bug bash, they still use the same sheet for logging of the bugs, but then how do we triage those bugs?

104
00:11:02,910 --> 00:11:05,390
How do we then import them into actual Jira?

105
00:11:05,390 --> 00:11:11,030
How do we prioritize these things for like what's really a blocker, non-blocker?

106
00:11:11,030 --> 00:11:19,310
So, some of these tasks my team has started to actually build agents that will help us with this kind of efficiency.

107
00:11:19,310 --> 00:11:24,470
This helps us a lot with multiple teams are having multiple bug bashes,

108
00:11:24,470 --> 00:11:31,630
and so this has reduced a lot of our manager and tech lead times in some of the process automation.

109
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:33,870
What is the difference between a and an agent?

110
00:11:33,880 --> 00:11:42,270
Yeah, skill is something that you can share with people who have similar problems.

111
00:11:42,270 --> 00:11:50,470
So, let's say if I look at certain dashboards, most of the senior EMs within the team have a similar kind of an ask, right?

112
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:52,710
To look at their own systems and their own dashboards.

113
00:11:52,710 --> 00:11:57,150
And if I write a skill, it's something that is easily shareable.

114
00:11:57,150 --> 00:12:03,350
It's the same thing with agent, but an agent is actually performing a task on your behalf.

115
00:12:03,350 --> 00:12:09,070
Skill is like you actually invoke it whenever you want.

116
00:12:09,070 --> 00:12:11,870
Um it's just doing summary and synthesizing and letting you know.

117
00:12:11,870 --> 00:12:20,110
You could build skills that are actually then become like composite skills could become an agent, right?

118
00:12:20,110 --> 00:12:26,350
And so, skill's a little bit more of an atomic unit for your own kind of a task, if you will.

119
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:28,070
So, easier to get started with skills.

120
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:32,670
Over time, you can compose multiple skills into agents.

121
00:12:32,680 --> 00:12:40,190
Yes. Do you also get AI's help maybe to suggest the skills based on what you do on a day-to-day basis?

122
00:12:40,200 --> 00:12:50,630
I actually have not used it to suggest skills because the way I was looking initially was I know a bunch of things that I don't like doing, right?

123
00:12:50,630 --> 00:12:53,829
Like it's just too much of a grind.

124
00:12:53,829 --> 00:12:59,790
I tried to have like an Eisenhower metrics in my head when I go in on a Monday, right?

125
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:01,829
What's urgent and important?

126
00:13:01,829 --> 00:13:08,510
What is like non-urgent and important, but I just don't have time to get to it, right?

127
00:13:08,510 --> 00:13:18,310
And so, that's kind of how I went into, "Okay, here's where I would love to have skills so that they are all important tasks, but I don't want them to become like this urgent thing."

128
00:13:18,310 --> 00:13:27,550
I am sure that now that I have used so much of Claude that Claude knows a lot about like how I think or how I do things.

129
00:13:27,550 --> 00:13:35,710
And over time, I'm sure I could just ask it for suggesting a skill, and it would start recommending some of these.

130
00:13:35,710 --> 00:13:38,870
But, I haven't yet started to use it in that mode.

131
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:45,790
But, it certainly seems like AI has changed the balance of time as a manager.

132
00:13:45,790 --> 00:13:47,670
You do some things less, and you do some things more.

133
00:13:47,670 --> 00:13:49,630
What is it that you are able to do more now?

134
00:13:49,640 --> 00:14:04,030
One particular thing that I definitely feel like I can do more is I have like lots of ideas about where my team should be investing in, what should we be working on.

135
00:14:04,030 --> 00:14:12,990
Sometimes it's product ideas, sometimes it's platform ideas, sometimes it's just ideas that are like I love to just explore, right?

136
00:14:12,990 --> 00:14:18,990
And what could be possible, what should we be doing, and things like that.

137
00:14:18,990 --> 00:14:26,590
I feel now I have lots [clears throat] of ideas into like these mini prototypes, if you will.

138
00:14:26,590 --> 00:14:32,270
And I feel like I don't have to spend a whole amount of time preparing presentations or decks,

139
00:14:32,270 --> 00:14:40,950
and like convincing my cross-functional partners or like my peers or my direct reports on to something,

140
00:14:40,950 --> 00:14:47,910
because I'm actually able to have a lot more solid prototype, if you will, to get them on the same page.

141
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:56,430
Now that we have talked a little bit about how your day-to-day has changed, let's talk about the team.

142
00:14:56,430 --> 00:15:03,270
As AI has become part of everyday engineering work, what did you have to change first?

143
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:12,590
Did you have to make sure that there was certain context in place, certain frameworks in place, to make the team effective with AI? Can we start with that?

144
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:19,390
So, I will say that we started this AI adoption around 9-10 months ago.

145
00:15:19,390 --> 00:15:26,990
It started with should we use cursor or WinSurf or

146
00:15:26,990 --> 00:15:33,270
GitHub Copilot to start augmenting to the tasks that we were doing.

147
00:15:33,270 --> 00:15:35,710
And we started to see with just adoption, right?

148
00:15:35,710 --> 00:15:44,870
How many people were actually adopting any of these AI tools that were becoming available say last August or September.

149
00:15:44,870 --> 00:15:52,230
And that's the only thing in my mind or I guess even in our leaders' mind is let's have people starting to adopt these things and

150
00:15:52,230 --> 00:15:57,990
then we will see if there is anything in there.

151
00:15:57,990 --> 00:16:07,590
I think the true adoption started happening with Claude Code coming out in early January and a lot of us using these tools during our hackathon week.

152
00:16:07,600 --> 00:16:11,510
And that was actually a truly transformational moment.

153
00:16:11,510 --> 00:16:16,910
I think personally for myself as well as for a bunch of people on my team.

154
00:16:16,910 --> 00:16:22,950
We used pretty much all the AI tools that were available out there.

155
00:16:22,950 --> 00:16:26,550
We used Replit for some app mockups.

156
00:16:26,550 --> 00:16:29,030
We used Claude Code for coding.

157
00:16:29,030 --> 00:16:32,070
We used Gemini for presentations.

158
00:16:32,070 --> 00:16:40,150
And I think that's where all of us started to feel a little bit like there is something here that's truly going to help us understand.

159
00:16:40,150 --> 00:16:49,670
And then I think in February and then after February I would say, our goal initially was let's have adoption metric, right?

160
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:52,870
Let's have everybody use it.

161
00:16:52,870 --> 00:16:55,829
Including us as managers.

162
00:16:55,829 --> 00:17:00,470
And once all of us started using it, we started sharing our learnings.

163
00:17:00,470 --> 00:17:07,670
There were some people who were early adopters and those early adopters actually showed us what all can be done.

164
00:17:07,670 --> 00:17:12,710
Like I talked about the bug bash agent or somebody else wrote some other agent.

165
00:17:12,710 --> 00:17:19,390
And that's kind of how we started sharing in our team share outs or

166
00:17:19,390 --> 00:17:26,870
our company shareout and we actually created an environment of learning through these shareouts and

167
00:17:26,870 --> 00:17:37,390
plugins and sharing skills and it became a little bit more infectious I guess that oh you're seeing something that's really valuable to your team.

168
00:17:37,390 --> 00:17:45,870
Why don't I use it in my team kind of a thing and that's when more team members starting to use it and

169
00:17:45,870 --> 00:17:52,150
now it's almost like everybody on the team is using AI tools for the code that they write or the tasks that they do.

170
00:17:52,160 --> 00:17:58,550
What do you see separating people that use the tools well versus others?

171
00:17:58,550 --> 00:18:02,190
Like what are the specific new skills that matter more?

172
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:09,750
I think what matters is sometimes when we have a lot of experience,

173
00:18:09,750 --> 00:18:16,590
I think our first way of doing a task is very much hard set in our minds because

174
00:18:16,590 --> 00:18:21,350
it feels like oh I know how to do this myself, right?

175
00:18:21,350 --> 00:18:30,270
And I'll take an example of my own self like when I get called into an on-call incident, I know what my steps are.

176
00:18:30,270 --> 00:18:33,190
It's almost like a muscle memory that's there.

177
00:18:33,190 --> 00:18:35,950
I know which dashboards to go,

178
00:18:35,950 --> 00:18:44,790
I know which logs to check, I know which regex to like actually look for for a certain kind of an error or a pattern and

179
00:18:44,790 --> 00:18:53,430
it's very easy for me to do it myself than actually saying oh looks like there is a new operational agent that's out there.

180
00:18:53,430 --> 00:19:01,550
Let me talk to that agent and let me try to explain to that agent what I'm looking for in the logs and

181
00:19:01,550 --> 00:19:08,470
I think for somebody who has a lot more experience, it's harder because your muscle memory, your things with your code base,

182
00:19:08,470 --> 00:19:14,830
the things that you know, it's easier almost to do it than to like ask for how to do it.

183
00:19:14,830 --> 00:19:23,910
But it's easier for people who have not implemented something, who are new on the on-call roster, if you will, for them to be able to know these things.

184
00:19:23,920 --> 00:19:31,110
And if they are alone without a tech lead, let's say at midnight, they get a phone call.

185
00:19:31,110 --> 00:19:36,990
For them, it's so much easier to be actually saying, "Hey, I My team owns this particular code base.

186
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:41,430
What are the kinds of errors that I should be looking for?

187
00:19:41,430 --> 00:19:48,030
Or what are you seeing as the errors that have exceeded P99s or P95s or whatever, right?

188
00:19:48,030 --> 00:19:54,390
And so, I feel like there is that little bit of asymmetry of knowledge that's there.

189
00:19:54,390 --> 00:20:02,710
So, it's again up to people for how they want to use it and how they can use it to up-level themselves.

190
00:20:02,710 --> 00:20:10,870
I have realized that now some of the tasks that are muscle memory for me, they are only within my repos and what I own.

191
00:20:10,870 --> 00:20:16,870
But I don't have that muscle memory for something that's across the boundary or across the org.

192
00:20:16,870 --> 00:20:20,990
And I actually feel like the agent is really helping me cross that boundary, right?

193
00:20:20,990 --> 00:20:31,990
Instead of calling that other team's on-call engineer, I can do some more debugging before I decide, "Okay, let me page somebody else."

194
00:20:31,990 --> 00:20:34,270
So, I feel like there is still a lot of learning, I will say.

195
00:20:34,270 --> 00:20:38,790
But at the same time, I feel like it's helping cross boundaries a little bit better.

196
00:20:38,800 --> 00:20:46,670
It's very interesting insight that you really have to unlearn certain things before you can use AI effectively.

197
00:20:46,680 --> 00:20:49,310
Yeah. This leads me to my next question.

198
00:20:49,310 --> 00:20:51,470
Let me explain that a little bit.

199
00:20:51,470 --> 00:20:57,150
Just taking your example of the on-call and somebody that's new at this,

200
00:20:57,150 --> 00:21:06,390
they're maybe firing off multiple threads with the AI to understand what might be going wrong.

201
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:09,670
Potentially multiple agents.

202
00:21:09,670 --> 00:21:15,070
So, is there a perspective you have with respect to the fact that

203
00:21:15,070 --> 00:21:26,950
the engineers themselves now have to gain some managerial capabilities in managing multiple threads or multiple agents as they do their day-to-day work.

204
00:21:26,950 --> 00:21:33,630
This is something I've heard that software engineers now are becoming managers of a team of agents.

205
00:21:33,630 --> 00:21:36,271
Do you see any truth to that in practice? From your experience?

206
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:43,470
I actually think that and maybe this is like my whole brain is into this whole system thinking, right?

207
00:21:43,470 --> 00:21:50,790
I think as engineers we always had this multitasking, multithreading kind of a mindset.

208
00:21:50,790 --> 00:21:57,830
So, there is a chance as like you are junior in your career, you don't know where to start and you like have three, four, five agents.

209
00:21:57,830 --> 00:22:03,430
Let's just take an on-call that they just fire out to see what will come back, right?

210
00:22:03,430 --> 00:22:06,470
But I'm a big believer of learning by doing.

211
00:22:06,480 --> 00:22:12,670
It's okay if somebody puts four, five agents as long as they are in their token count, I guess.

212
00:22:12,670 --> 00:22:14,910
I'm okay with whatever they're doing.

213
00:22:14,910 --> 00:22:17,190
But I think you learn over time, right?

214
00:22:17,190 --> 00:22:27,710
Like you learn that okay, all these multithreaded architectures, they could all like lead to too many garbage collections or dumps and things like that.

215
00:22:27,710 --> 00:22:38,710
And so, I think what's going to happen or this is like maybe my speculation a little bit here, but I do think that it's going to help us up-level ourselves faster, right?

216
00:22:38,710 --> 00:22:44,470
We will actually onboard a little bit faster.

217
00:22:44,470 --> 00:22:53,310
We will actually get to that depth a little bit faster if we just are not afraid of failing through having multiple agents and learning.

218
00:22:53,310 --> 00:22:57,470
I think the most important thing is are you curious and are you learning?

219
00:22:57,470 --> 00:23:03,150
Are you actually reading the output of these agents?

220
00:23:03,150 --> 00:23:09,750
Are you understanding why like two three agents conflicted with each other and actually produce something that wasn't the right outcome, right?

221
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:20,190
And as long as people are willing to learn, I think it's okay to learn by failing.

222
00:23:20,190 --> 00:23:29,951
I do think that people are learning and adapting faster given the fact that they are understanding that okay, maybe this was a direction that didn't lead an outcome. Right?

223
00:23:29,951 --> 00:23:32,230
So, okay, this is not how I should think.

224
00:23:32,230 --> 00:23:38,630
The next task they are like, okay, I just need one or two things that I need agents for.

225
00:23:38,630 --> 00:23:45,430
So, I think like that learning I'm seeing happening within my team and I think it's going to happen even in the broader industry.

226
00:23:45,440 --> 00:23:50,110
Maybe I can re-ask a question with a potentially better example.

227
00:23:50,110 --> 00:23:59,950
Now that a lot of the work is done by the AI agents, the coding agent, for example, or testing agent if somebody's created it.

228
00:23:59,950 --> 00:24:07,670
Are the more effective engineers able to take on more projects at the same time because they're able to manage these multiple trends or agents?

229
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:10,670
I think the answer is definitely yes.

230
00:24:10,680 --> 00:24:20,190
From like a building perspective, they are able to take on multiple tasks or multiple projects.

231
00:24:20,190 --> 00:24:23,950
I think the places where

232
00:24:23,950 --> 00:24:32,950
I still am yet to see the outcomes is are all of these things in the right direction and are we going to have like 3x more throughput and things like that?

233
00:24:32,960 --> 00:24:39,270
I think that is still something depends on how complex is your architecture,

234
00:24:39,270 --> 00:24:50,270
how complex is some of the things that you depend on other teams and how distributed, how much of legacy, how much of new build that you have.

235
00:24:50,270 --> 00:24:55,230
And so, I think the answer is there is somewhere in the middle,

236
00:24:55,230 --> 00:25:01,990
but you definitely see that lot of engineers are able to take on more building tasks and

237
00:25:01,990 --> 00:25:11,070
a lot of time is spent on code reviews, deployments, operational excellence, hard debugging, testing of some of these projects.

238
00:25:11,070 --> 00:25:13,910
So, like the time to build is shrinking.

239
00:25:13,910 --> 00:25:22,310
The other parts are like you have to now figure out how do you optimize the deployment cycles, the test cycles, and other things on the operation side.

240
00:25:22,320 --> 00:25:31,910
Let's now spend some time in how you talk to the team, how do you coach the team, what are you hearing from the team as everybody is using more of AI?

241
00:25:31,920 --> 00:25:39,150
Perhaps, could you share an example of a specific one-on-one where AI changed the conversation in some way?

242
00:25:39,160 --> 00:25:53,230
Yeah, I had a skip level one-on-one with somebody who had just joined the team, very young, early in career, and we were just having a conversation of how these new AI tools are helping.

243
00:25:53,230 --> 00:25:59,310
And one of the questions very organically came up was, "Hey, how do you get evaluated?"

244
00:25:59,310 --> 00:26:11,230
And I talked about like, you know, the metrics that I get evaluated on because this is a new engineer, he wants to know how his skip or whatever gets evaluated in their performance reviews and stuff.

245
00:26:11,230 --> 00:26:13,550
Which I thought was a very nice question to ask.

246
00:26:13,560 --> 00:26:21,990
And I talked about the fact that from a quality perspective, I get rated on triage rate of our team.

247
00:26:21,990 --> 00:26:25,510
And this person asked me, "What is that metric now?"

248
00:26:25,510 --> 00:26:34,030
And I said, "Looks like our triage rate across all the teams that report into me seems to be 5 days."

249
00:26:34,030 --> 00:26:36,550
And he was like, "Why is it 5 days?"

250
00:26:36,550 --> 00:26:42,830
And I'm like, "Because we have this process and this process and then like it goes through like all of these steps."

251
00:26:42,830 --> 00:26:48,830
And this person went back, looked at all the flows,

252
00:26:48,830 --> 00:26:58,470
and actually built a triage agent that reduced our triage time across all the tickets that we had to 9 hours, right?

253
00:26:58,470 --> 00:27:04,990
And just the realization that I had was having just a conversation on something that

254
00:27:04,990 --> 00:27:13,950
I get evaluated on and what the published metric was, someone was able to think about how can we make that better, right?

255
00:27:13,950 --> 00:27:16,150
And it's not that we didn't have triage tools before.

256
00:27:16,150 --> 00:27:17,750
We did have triage tools.

257
00:27:17,750 --> 00:27:20,510
They were very text-based triage tools.

258
00:27:20,510 --> 00:27:30,830
This person was able to actually figure out how do we add visualizer to this triage tool, what kinds of other things that we could add to make this much better.

259
00:27:30,830 --> 00:27:36,870
And so I feel like some of these conversations and one-on-ones have changed.

260
00:27:36,870 --> 00:27:41,710
It's also like up-leveling and inspiring other people on the team to think bigger.

261
00:27:41,710 --> 00:27:51,670
And the things that they didn't think about were possible before, everybody feels now that things are possible or at least making an attempt to make them possible.

262
00:27:51,680 --> 00:27:55,790
So that's certainly a very positive example.

263
00:27:55,790 --> 00:27:58,030
Have you had any negative interaction on the use of AI?

264
00:27:58,030 --> 00:28:03,070
I mean, there's certainly a lot of negative emotions associated with that as well.

265
00:28:03,080 --> 00:28:07,350
Yeah, of course there are negatives, right?

266
00:28:07,350 --> 00:28:09,630
This is at the end we're actually building software.

267
00:28:09,630 --> 00:28:15,550
There are a lot of things like we also go through where we have made mistakes as humans, right?

268
00:28:15,550 --> 00:28:25,030
I have written code in production that caused a production issue when I was a junior engineer and have those things definitely happen.

269
00:28:25,030 --> 00:28:32,230
One example that I had was we tried to integrate an agent within our on-call alerts.

270
00:28:32,230 --> 00:28:40,390
And I got paged at 3:00 a.m. for this particular alert and I tried to use the agent to debug it and

271
00:28:40,390 --> 00:28:48,550
the results were so all over the place that for almost an hour I felt like I was misguided by its investigation because

272
00:28:48,550 --> 00:28:53,190
of the hallucinations that the agent had and not understanding the system.

273
00:28:53,190 --> 00:28:57,350
And I literally had to say, "Okay, I'm not going to look at this output.

274
00:28:57,350 --> 00:29:04,750
I'm going to use my first principles thinking and I'm going to go through the stack one by one to figure out where the problem is."

275
00:29:04,760 --> 00:29:09,390
And I think my engineers are also facing some of these things.

276
00:29:09,390 --> 00:29:20,790
There is definitely a chance that there will be things that are not good and you have to constantly challenge things from a first principles basis.

277
00:29:20,790 --> 00:29:27,310
Don't just take the output of the agents and assume that it's all like the greatest output.

278
00:29:27,310 --> 00:29:37,350
Use your first principles thinking, your systems thinking, especially if you're working on critical systems, critical problems.

279
00:29:37,350 --> 00:29:47,550
They have to have a certain level of rigor and we cannot lose this first principles system design way of thinking about some of these outputs.

280
00:29:47,560 --> 00:29:59,150
So, with that, with coding becoming faster, with designs outputs becoming potentially faster, and you mentioned review is critical.

281
00:29:59,150 --> 00:30:02,030
Now, is review the new bottleneck?

282
00:30:02,030 --> 00:30:11,790
Do you have to really treat that as a very first class work instead of something, I believe, you know, we've thought of engineers do reviews also.

283
00:30:11,800 --> 00:30:22,550
Yeah, actually we'll talk about a funny situation where I mentioned to one of my senior most ICs how the joy of coding has come back in like managers' life.

284
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:30,630
And this person says, "And the joy of code reviews is taken away from my life."

285
00:30:30,630 --> 00:30:34,830
And so, it was a very funny banter, but you are spot-on.

286
00:30:34,830 --> 00:30:37,030
Reviews are the big bottleneck right now.

287
00:30:37,030 --> 00:30:45,550
There is a lot and lot of pressure on senior most ICs to make sure that they are holding that

288
00:30:45,550 --> 00:30:53,110
gate and the bar and the guardrail and figuring out what level of automation is needed so that reviews don't become a bottleneck.

289
00:30:53,110 --> 00:31:00,430
Because the expectation is that we will have meaningful impact with lot more experimentation, lot more throughput.

290
00:31:00,440 --> 00:31:08,990
And so right now one of the biggest challenges is figuring out the reviews, the deployments, the testing cycles.

291
00:31:08,990 --> 00:31:21,070
How do we do meaningful innovation on those aspects of software development and test life cycles so that we are shipping scalable products.

292
00:31:21,080 --> 00:31:24,630
So at this point in time,

293
00:31:24,630 --> 00:31:31,870
do you see real evidence that the same team can do more or

294
00:31:31,870 --> 00:31:37,150
has the work really shifted into figuring out how to do better reviews and provide oversight and validations?

295
00:31:37,160 --> 00:31:42,510
I'll tell you the things that I feel have become easier to build.

296
00:31:42,510 --> 00:31:53,550
I think operations, like when you are building like APIs, you have always have this create, read, update, delete kinds of CRUD APIs, right?

297
00:31:53,550 --> 00:31:56,510
Building those things has become really easy.

298
00:31:56,510 --> 00:32:06,190
There are things within the system design of something like oh this needs an offline compute, this needs some kind of a nearline thing.

299
00:32:06,190 --> 00:32:08,990
This needs like a pub/sub architecture.

300
00:32:08,990 --> 00:32:13,910
All of those parts of the build that design thinking, that upfront thinking about how do you want to think,

301
00:32:13,920 --> 00:32:19,390
how do you want to break out certain architectures,

302
00:32:19,390 --> 00:32:30,030
I do think that that it has given us more time to think about some of the things upfront and it has helped with like reducing this CRUD kind of work that we used to do.

303
00:32:30,030 --> 00:32:41,350
I think even in the review side of things, I will say that we do have like automation review stuff which does a great job on like the crud APIs and that those reviews, right?

304
00:32:41,360 --> 00:32:47,710
The reviews where things are a little bit complicated is just like the context of the whole architecture and stuff.

305
00:32:47,710 --> 00:32:50,790
So, I do see the build velocity has gone up.

306
00:32:50,800 --> 00:33:01,110
I think the testing deployment infrastructural side of things are the ones where we're still not seeing as much gain.

307
00:33:01,110 --> 00:33:03,390
It's helping with debugging, I would say, right?

308
00:33:03,390 --> 00:33:08,710
Like when you're doing some offline compute if like your data jobs or whatever are failing,

309
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:23,470
definitely have AI helping us debug something faster than we would have done before, but that's an area where it's not like it it understands our own data, data lineage, data policies, and things like that.

310
00:33:23,470 --> 00:33:26,510
And so, we definitely have not seen gains in that area.

311
00:33:26,510 --> 00:33:30,070
But, crud side of things, we definitely have seen a lot of gain.

312
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:33,350
Are you measuring productivity?

313
00:33:33,350 --> 00:33:39,750
I've heard of token usage discussed as a metric.

314
00:33:39,750 --> 00:33:42,830
Is that useful or is that really really measuring effort and activity?

315
00:33:42,840 --> 00:33:45,110
What other metrics are you using?

316
00:33:45,120 --> 00:33:53,670
We are going through a lot of different ways of thinking about developer productivity.

317
00:33:53,670 --> 00:33:56,990
I think for adoption and usage, we definitely look at token.

318
00:33:57,000 --> 00:34:08,990
Again, token usage tells you that everyone who is adopting is figuring out whether to use it in a plan mode, whether to use it in an execution or an agent mode.

319
00:34:08,990 --> 00:34:12,950
And so, it tells you a little bit of the adoption, I will say.

320
00:34:12,960 --> 00:34:18,270
The other parts of the metrics are how often is your trunk getting blocked?

321
00:34:18,270 --> 00:34:23,869
You know, how often are like PRs getting reverted and things like that.

322
00:34:23,869 --> 00:34:31,190
So, all of those metrics are something that I am actually pushing the team to start measuring, right?

323
00:34:31,190 --> 00:34:34,149
And not just have like the token usage.

324
00:34:34,149 --> 00:34:43,110
Token usage kind of tells you a little bit more horizontal like adoption metric, if you will, but not really a throughput metric.

325
00:34:43,110 --> 00:34:51,230
And so, the throughput metric comes with the number of experiments that the team is able to do, the number of initiatives the team is now able to ship,

326
00:34:51,230 --> 00:34:57,670
the number of times that has reduced self-inflicted incidents, if you will.

327
00:34:57,670 --> 00:35:06,510
So, those are some of the metrics that I'm actually pushing for, because that actually give you a little bit more about the system health.

328
00:35:06,520 --> 00:35:12,910
Are you also measuring just how much of the code is written by AI at this time?

329
00:35:12,920 --> 00:35:21,270
We do have all the adoption metrics have the lines AI produced lines of code, token usage, like you said.

330
00:35:21,270 --> 00:35:25,670
All of those things are definitely part of the adoption metric of the team.

331
00:35:25,670 --> 00:35:32,830
The system metrics and stuff is also something that I'm keeping an eye on, right?

332
00:35:32,830 --> 00:35:37,790
Which is making sure that with this AI metrics going up, the system metrics can't go down.

333
00:35:37,790 --> 00:35:41,310
Like they have to stay complementing each other.

334
00:35:41,320 --> 00:35:46,110
So, I've also heard about people saying we are now shipping more story points.

335
00:35:46,120 --> 00:35:50,670
In which category does of metrics for you does this fall under?

336
00:35:50,680 --> 00:35:55,430
It falls under the number of initiatives the team is able to ship, right?

337
00:35:55,430 --> 00:35:59,470
When you talk about the number of story points, what were story points?

338
00:35:59,470 --> 00:36:02,910
Story points was our like estimates, right?

339
00:36:02,910 --> 00:36:05,270
As developers, we were always optimistic.

340
00:36:05,280 --> 00:36:17,750
We would give some estimates and say that oh, we will be able to deliver 10 story points a week, if you were a four people team or something like that.

341
00:36:17,750 --> 00:36:23,510
And now, you know, with AI tools, people get just come up with another estimate, right?

342
00:36:23,510 --> 00:36:31,430
I feel like that guestimate was also something that we never really like understood it very well,

343
00:36:31,430 --> 00:36:40,950
but I think right now I feel it's a lot more concrete in terms of how much kind of actually gets shipped and rolled out 200% right?

344
00:36:40,950 --> 00:36:51,270
And that is really the meaningful impact rather than just looking at Kanban boards and looking at like story points and velocity and story points.

345
00:36:51,270 --> 00:36:57,470
I think those were all measures for us to be able to flag risks early on,

346
00:36:57,470 --> 00:37:03,350
but I feel the number the OKRs that you take on as a manager to whether it's product OKRs or

347
00:37:03,360 --> 00:37:13,310
engineering OKRs and how many do you ship and roll 200% production is like the new way of measuring the throughput.

348
00:37:13,320 --> 00:37:19,630
So while we're on the topic on the craft, I would like to ask you about the methodologies.

349
00:37:19,630 --> 00:37:26,310
You did talk about the fact that you do have story points measured today and Kanban for certain use cases.

350
00:37:26,310 --> 00:37:34,870
Is the methodology of Agile Scrum that we have followed for the last several years still relevant in the age of AI?

351
00:37:34,870 --> 00:37:37,391
What do you see as of now? What do you think?

352
00:37:37,400 --> 00:37:43,310
I think it's changing even with Agile, right?

353
00:37:43,310 --> 00:37:45,870
Every team used it in their own different ways, right?

354
00:37:45,870 --> 00:37:57,590
Some teams still did like a lot more waterfall upfront thinking and then only when it went into the build cycle did they follow like some Agile practices.

355
00:37:57,590 --> 00:38:02,910
In some cases, I come from startups and I remember we did everything from like Agile, right?

356
00:38:02,910 --> 00:38:09,390
Like your product design, engineers, everybody was thinking about story points.

357
00:38:09,390 --> 00:38:12,030
We didn't even know what MVP was going to look like.

358
00:38:12,030 --> 00:38:17,430
And so I feel like all of these Agile practices that are there before,

359
00:38:17,430 --> 00:38:28,470
they are still going to be valid in terms of how teams curate them for their own use cases or outcomes that they want.

360
00:38:28,470 --> 00:38:34,910
I would say one thing that will change is everybody will be a builder now, right?

361
00:38:34,910 --> 00:38:41,470
So, before in your Agile teams, you had PMs and designs who were doing certain aspects of the tasks,

362
00:38:41,480 --> 00:38:49,071
but they were not building with you together, but now those boundaries are gone. They could be building with you.

363
00:38:49,080 --> 00:38:59,350
They could be writing like let's say product policies and things within the code themselves, and it's not like you have to translate it in some ways,

364
00:38:59,350 --> 00:39:04,430
and so I feel like now the story points are even going to be a bigger set because

365
00:39:04,440 --> 00:39:16,470
it's going to be augmented with all the different job functions together than some of the ways where only engineers were part of that story point calculations.

366
00:39:16,480 --> 00:39:19,230
Interesting.

367
00:39:19,230 --> 00:39:21,830
So, does that you believe change the composition of the team itself?

368
00:39:21,840 --> 00:39:24,070
I think it will change.

369
00:39:24,070 --> 00:39:31,990
It's not changing yet, but if I look at what I am seeing now,

370
00:39:31,990 --> 00:39:42,510
I do believe that, you know, in a year or two, the boundaries of who builds what is definitely going to be changing.

371
00:39:42,510 --> 00:39:44,630
And so, the composition of the Agile teams will change.

372
00:39:44,630 --> 00:39:52,710
The compositions of how we build and ship things will change, and how we actually use things will also probably change.

373
00:39:52,720 --> 00:39:59,030
So, perhaps that's a good segue into hiring and onboarding.

374
00:39:59,030 --> 00:40:02,350
With AI, you're looking for different things when you're hiring.

375
00:40:02,360 --> 00:40:13,190
I think in terms of our modules that we use for hiring, the only one that we have added is AI native module,

376
00:40:13,190 --> 00:40:22,310
which is what somebody's ways of using an AI tool while they are thinking of solving a problem.

377
00:40:22,310 --> 00:40:25,630
So, that's the one change that we have done.

378
00:40:25,630 --> 00:40:28,830
Again, the way to kind of grade this module,

379
00:40:28,830 --> 00:40:34,910
we are learning and calibrating amongst ourselves as we kind of understand different people and

380
00:40:34,920 --> 00:40:44,030
different people's ways of using that AI tool, but that's one change that we have started to take into consideration.

381
00:40:44,030 --> 00:40:50,790
I think the other part is I do spend a lot of time in my interviews,

382
00:40:50,790 --> 00:41:00,230
which are a lot more communications-wise, on somebody's curiosity and adaptability and willingness to learn any new skill.

383
00:41:00,240 --> 00:41:07,950
And it doesn't matter whether it's related to coding, debugging, just being a team player, right?

384
00:41:07,950 --> 00:41:16,310
I think like those are some of the things that I have started to ask a lot more questions on than I would before.

385
00:41:16,310 --> 00:41:22,310
So, those are the changes that I'm doing in hiring side of things.

386
00:41:22,310 --> 00:41:27,230
In terms of onboarding, again, we used to initially have this onboarding tasks.

387
00:41:27,230 --> 00:41:30,150
Were very simple tasks, right?

388
00:41:30,150 --> 00:41:35,390
Like, "Hey, after your dev boot camp, just pick up like one or two backlog bugs."

389
00:41:35,390 --> 00:41:40,030
But now those bugs have become like smaller automation tasks, right?

390
00:41:40,030 --> 00:41:47,990
So, in terms of how are we onboarding, we are actually onboarding by doing a lot more than what we used to have onboarding tasks before.

391
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:53,070
Do you have expectations around new hire?

392
00:41:53,070 --> 00:42:01,550
Do you set expectations around what is acceptable AI use and how should they consider security and even ownership of the output?

393
00:42:01,560 --> 00:42:14,430
In terms of onboarding, we still use the same 30-60-90 day template of what is expected out of your performance at the end

394
00:42:14,430 --> 00:42:17,750
of 30 days, 60 days, 90 days in terms of AI adoption.

395
00:42:17,750 --> 00:42:25,190
Again, like I said, in the first 30 days, I'm not looking at them to be like a power user, but at end of 60 days,

396
00:42:25,190 --> 00:42:31,470
I'm looking at them to be wide and using all of the AI tools that the team is using, right?

397
00:42:31,470 --> 00:42:34,150
For doing whatever task.

398
00:42:34,150 --> 00:42:41,630
At the end of 90 days, you definitely want them to take ownership of the outcome that they are producing, right?

399
00:42:41,630 --> 00:42:47,110
And so that's something that I don't think that it has changed because of AI.

400
00:42:47,110 --> 00:42:54,470
I think it's just the fact that you want that the maybe the complexity of the task has become a little bit bigger of what they own,

401
00:42:54,470 --> 00:42:58,790
but I think my expectation is that at the end of 90 days,

402
00:42:58,790 --> 00:43:09,790
you should feel like you're part of the team and you whatever you build, you own it and it's something that you will continue to take the ownership for.

403
00:43:09,790 --> 00:43:13,830
When hiring and you did talk about everybody on the team becomes a builder.

404
00:43:13,830 --> 00:43:22,830
Will continue look for specialist perhaps in you know, in their different areas, perhaps specialist in architecture, specialist in platform support or UX.

405
00:43:22,840 --> 00:43:26,030
I think I will.

406
00:43:26,030 --> 00:43:37,310
I think each of the job function brings a different creativity to the table and so it's very important that you have a team that has those creative thought process.

407
00:43:37,310 --> 00:43:45,710
Like I said, I come from back end engineering and there are very lot of times when I actually don't see difference between square corners and rounded corners, right?

408
00:43:45,710 --> 00:43:58,230
But I have engineers on my team who have that UX specialty who are able to actually see these things and can actually say here's what a world-class UX looks like.

409
00:43:58,230 --> 00:44:04,950
And so I do think that there is the specialist part of it is still very,

410
00:44:04,950 --> 00:44:15,590
very important because our human judgment, our human way of evaluating what good looks like, what fair looks like, what great looks like, cannot be taken over by an agent, right?

411
00:44:15,600 --> 00:44:19,350
And so, I think even when I'm looking at architects,

412
00:44:19,350 --> 00:44:31,030
I mean, there are still so many times where I get called into the architecture reviews and I'm like blown away by some paper that they will talk about or they'll talk about, "Here's how we should be scaling."

413
00:44:31,030 --> 00:44:33,910
Or, "Here's kind of what the new GPU map looks like."

414
00:44:33,910 --> 00:44:36,670
And so,

415
00:44:36,670 --> 00:44:43,110
I do think that there is that specialty of architects and the experience and

416
00:44:43,110 --> 00:44:50,190
things that comes with experience that is important in a team and in building a successful team.

417
00:44:50,190 --> 00:44:54,390
So, I would say that, you know, all of those specialties exist.

418
00:44:54,400 --> 00:45:07,750
In my mind, that does match with your earlier comment that while AI can help you with a lot, you still have to have that understanding and the specialized experience does help you with understanding is probably your point.

419
00:45:07,760 --> 00:45:09,630
Yes, absolutely.

420
00:45:09,640 --> 00:45:21,110
Let's start to wrap up now and my first question as we trying to wind down would be, you've talked about how AI has changed your day-to-day,

421
00:45:21,110 --> 00:45:26,910
some of your discussions with your team, the work done on your team.

422
00:45:26,910 --> 00:45:30,710
What about your peers and your manager? How has AI changed that interaction?

423
00:45:30,720 --> 00:45:43,670
I think one good thing is all of my peers and managers, we all participated in that hackathon and went for a manager coding camp.

424
00:45:43,670 --> 00:45:48,430
And I think that actually set like the level of like, "Hey, here's kind of what we can do together."

425
00:45:48,430 --> 00:45:52,910
Or, "Here's something that I can do now more."

426
00:45:52,910 --> 00:45:58,670
Or, like the whole exchange of thoughts and what we could do really, really helped.

427
00:45:58,670 --> 00:46:01,430
I think I mentioned this to somebody recently.

428
00:46:01,430 --> 00:46:10,190
What makes us managers in technology is because we were technical leads and technologists before, right?

429
00:46:10,190 --> 00:46:15,750
And so right now, we need to go and explore that part of the technologist or

430
00:46:15,750 --> 00:46:23,590
the tech lead that we used to be by actually using these tools, by actually doing some of the tasks that are on your team's plate,

431
00:46:23,590 --> 00:46:30,910
and getting that level of understanding so that you actually can have good conversations with your leaders,

432
00:46:30,910 --> 00:46:36,870
you can have good conversations with your peers, you can actually figure out what is possible and what is not possible.

433
00:46:36,870 --> 00:46:44,830
Are we all having the same problems of like code reviews or deployments or like whatever else, right?

434
00:46:44,830 --> 00:46:47,870
Like we can share and gain from each other.

435
00:46:47,870 --> 00:46:59,751
And so if there is one call to action for any of the engineering managers out there is don't have this anxiety because of AI about your job. Just learn by doing it.

436
00:46:59,760 --> 00:47:14,310
You don't need to have the same throughput as your topmost IC on the team, but you should learn so that you can walk in their shoes, you can have empathy to their problems, and you are the spokesperson for them.

437
00:47:14,320 --> 00:47:24,030
Is there anything you'd like to share about any postings you follow, any people you follow, or any podcasts that you follow?

438
00:47:24,030 --> 00:47:26,310
How do you keep up with what's changing every day?

439
00:47:26,320 --> 00:47:35,390
I actually just listen to I think the Anthropic's engineering blogs and stuff.

440
00:47:35,390 --> 00:47:40,670
I will be honest, I don't listen to a lot of podcasts,

441
00:47:40,670 --> 00:47:47,630
but I'm always looking at what Anthropic is doing in terms of their technical evolution and stuff.

442
00:47:47,630 --> 00:47:53,390
So that's one thing that I definitely keep up with to understand.

443
00:47:53,390 --> 00:47:58,550
I do attend like lot of I don't learn by watching trainings, if you will.

444
00:47:58,550 --> 00:48:07,150
I like to do like dabble myself and like get stuck and figure things out rather than somebody shows me how to do it.

445
00:48:07,150 --> 00:48:12,270
I don't think I learn through watching YouTube videos or just taking a learning course or whatever.

446
00:48:12,270 --> 00:48:15,910
I do a lot of like blogs and tech blogs reading.

447
00:48:15,920 --> 00:48:19,310
Anything we missed that you'd like to cover?

448
00:48:19,310 --> 00:48:23,990
You had a very specific call out to all managers is, you know, actually try the tools that your team's using.

449
00:48:24,000 --> 00:48:26,350
Anything else you'd like to talk about?

450
00:48:26,360 --> 00:48:35,510
I think outside of that, I know that there is a lot of anxiety, lot of fear going on.

451
00:48:35,510 --> 00:48:42,630
I think one thing which is truly comes from like my experience is keep the human aspect up and running.

452
00:48:42,640 --> 00:48:50,790
Like make sure that your human judgment is going to be supremely important in the products that you build.

453
00:48:50,790 --> 00:49:00,710
And so it's important that you don't get carried into this thing that you lose that human judgment, human intuition.

454
00:49:00,710 --> 00:49:03,350
Ask a lot of questions,

455
00:49:03,350 --> 00:49:12,550
build the muscle to examine things deeply so that you are also growing in your own understanding of how you should be building,

456
00:49:12,550 --> 00:49:19,990
how should you think about the business, how you should think about adoption of the products and platforms or scaling.

457
00:49:19,990 --> 00:49:21,870
But that's the only other thing that I would say.

458
00:49:21,870 --> 00:49:26,590
Like be human, be empathetic, use these tools.

459
00:49:26,590 --> 00:49:30,550
Don't get carried away with this anxiety and what you hear outside.

460
00:49:30,560 --> 00:49:33,724
That's nice to hear, Sonali. Not said enough, I think.

461
00:49:33,734 --> 00:49:33,990
[laughter]

462
00:49:34,000 --> 00:49:37,150
How can people follow your work or get in touch?

463
00:49:37,160 --> 00:49:39,870
Yeah, they can add me on LinkedIn.

464
00:49:39,880 --> 00:49:48,390
LinkedIn is a platform that I use a lot to connect to all professionals, so will be very happy to connect with people who want to get connected.

465
00:49:48,400 --> 00:50:02,230
Thank you so much for coming on the show and sharing where you are in the journey and I know it's early but sounds like there's a lot of learning that you have shared here and good luck as well.

466
00:50:02,240 --> 00:50:03,854
Thank you.

